# Line endings. The archive is a byte-for-byte record, so text is normalised to
# LF both in the repository and in the working tree, on any platform. Without
# this, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf would silently change .lean files
# and the tree would stop matching its own manifest.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Stated explicitly rather than relying on auto-detection, so the intent survives
# a future change to the catch-all rule above.
*.lean text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.sha256 text eol=lf

# The published distribution: fourteen binary chunks, 234 MB in total. These are
# the bytes that must never change, so they live in Git LFS and `-text` keeps any
# end-of-line or diff filter away from them.
Archive/Distribution/Part/lean.tar.gz.part* filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# The assembled archive, should it ever be tracked. It is ignored by default
# because it is derived from the parts above by concatenation.
*.tar.gz filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# The publication is 245 KB, ordinary blob territory. Keeping it out of LFS means
# a reader can clone and open the PDF without the LFS client, which matters for
# the one file most people come here for. Marked binary so no filter touches it.
*.pdf -text
